EldenNet, the spikes S0-A to S0-D (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs the four checks that come before EldenNet's first release in the game (v0.1): the spikes S0-A to
S0-D. They no longer depend on a call into the game: since the read-only rounds, EldenNet's start takes the places it
needs from its own table for your game's build (Lodestar's table, in gal\layouts) and checks each one there, and it
reads the game from a thread of its own. These runs check that start, on your PC, with keyboard and mouse:

  S0-A  The untouched game. EldenNet starts in the game as it runs, with nothing of the game changed and nothing of
        ours hooked into it, and checks each place of its table: that it lies where its kind must, and that the
        places in the game's code that use it still do. It checks them again once its start is over, to see that
        nothing changed them. Twenty starts loaded early, as the game starts, and twenty loaded late, at the title
        screen; the launcher starts and closes the game by itself each time.
  S0-B  The reader. EldenNet reads the game from a thread of its own, about ten times a second, for two hours of
        your play, and counts every reading it had to drop or could not make.
  S0-C  Offline play. The launcher starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat, in both of its two ways, and
        counts the game's connections to anything but this PC.
  S0-D  Guarded reads. EldenNet makes some of its own reads fail on purpose and must catch every one, alone and
        beside a stand-in for another program that watches for such faults; then five starts in which the launcher
        asks EldenNet to crash the game on purpose, each of which must be recorded.

What to expect today. The table for your build (2.7.2.0) is not complete yet: one place (the object that keeps the
save slot in use) and 36 of the fields EldenNet reads are still to be found by the next rounds. So every start ends
where it must, at EldenNet's banner "discovery failed", once it has checked what the table holds. That is not a
failure of any spike, and the launcher says so in plain words each time:

  S0-A  runs whole: a start that stops at the banner counts as it should, and the table's places are checked.
  S0-B  cannot run yet: the launcher says "The start's layout is not complete yet: S0-B waits for it." within a
        minute or two. Quit the game then; nothing is counted against it. Run it all the same: it shows that the
        start stops where it should.
  S0-C  runs as far as it can: the offline start and the connections are measured; the check of EldenNet's online
        gate waits for the layout.
  S0-D  runs as far as it can: the forced faults in EldenNet's own reads, and the crashes on purpose, are measured;
        the part that needs the reader (its breakers) waits for the layout.

EldenNet only reads. It calls nothing in the game, adds nothing to it and changes nothing in it, in every run. The
panel beside the game is a window of the launcher's own, outside the game. Nothing in this package is for playing
with yet.

Built from EldenNet's engine branch at commit @COMMIT@ with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you
alone, labelled @LABEL@.


How long it takes
-----------------

About two hours in all today, most of it unattended:

  S0-A  about 30 minutes for each of its two runs (twenty starts each), unattended: you can leave the PC.
  S0-B  a few minutes today (it stops at the title); two hours of play once the table is complete.
  S0-C  about 10 minutes for each of its two runs.
  S0-D  about 15 minutes for each of its two sessions, and about 10 minutes, unattended, for the five crashes.

Each run stands alone: you can stop after any of them and send what you have.


What is in it
-------------

  run-S0A-early.cmd, run-S0A-late.cmd, run-S0B.cmd, run-S0C-file.cmd, run-S0C-env.cmd, run-S0D.cmd, run-S0D-veh.cmd,
  run-S0D-crash.cmd
                          One file for each run: it starts eldennet-launcher.exe from this folder with that run's
                          options, then eldennet-engine.exe's spike-report on the files the runs have left so far. It
                          names nothing outside this folder but EldenNet's own folder, %APPDATA%\EldenNet (open one in
                          Notepad to read it).
  eldennet-launcher.exe   EldenNet's launcher: backs up your saves, starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat,
                          loads EldenNet, shows the panel of the run's tasks, and watches the game until it ends.
  eldennet.dll            EldenNet in the game: the probe build, which accepts the table's places while they are
                          still being checked, and makes S0-D's faults. It reads; it calls nothing in the game.
  eldennet-engine.exe     EldenNet's command-line tool: spike-report, which applies each spike's pass rules, and
                          lint, which checks a report.
  vehprobe.dll            A small program of ours that stands in for a crash reporter: it watches for faults in the
                          game's process and lets each one pass on. Only run S0D-veh loads it.
  gal\                    What EldenNet works from: the build's fingerprint (builds.toml), its own numbers and labels
                          for the places it needs (ids.toml), how a place is recognised (recipes.toml, with its note in
                          derivations\), what to look for (leads.toml) and the table for your build (layouts\).
                          Numbers and names of the design's own, nothing taken from the game.
  README-SPIKES.txt       This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside the programs.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game, and start Steam.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, anywhere but the game's folder.
  - Nothing is to be set up: no settings file to write, nothing to copy into the game's folder. If you have a
    %APPDATA%\EldenNet\config\settings.toml of your own, leave it as it is. If the GraceUI folder from the earlier
    spikes is still in the game's Game folder, EldenNet loads it: leave it there or delete it, either works (Pause
    still confirms the panel's tasks).
  - The launcher backs up your saves before each start (in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups). If you want a copy of
    your own as well, copy the folder %APPDATA%\EldenRing somewhere first.
  - The game will run offline, without Easy Anti-Cheat. Use a save you are happy to play offline, and never take a save
    played with mods into online play.
  - Set the game to windowed or borderless windowed mode first (in its graphics settings): the panel is a window of
    its own and a full-screen game covers it. It sits at the top right of the screen the game is on; drag it by its
    top line if it is in the way.
  - The launcher's messages appear in a console window behind the game, in lines starting with "EldenNet S0-A:" (or
    S0-B, and so on). Press Alt+Tab to look, and Alt+Tab back.
  - Windows or your security software may warn about an unsigned program. Allow it only if the SHA-256 values match
    (below); otherwise stop and tell us.


The steps
---------

Run them in this order. For each run, double-click its file in this folder (or open a command prompt here and type
its name; in PowerShell, put .\ in front: .\run-S0A-early.cmd). The console window opens and the game starts. The first
time, the launcher asks where eldenring.exe is if it cannot find it through Steam, and shows its notices. Follow the
panel beside the game: a waiting task counts down and ends by itself; a task of yours waits until you press Pause
(anywhere, while the game has the keyboard) or click the panel's button; the last task, quitting the game, has no
button. Once the game has ended, the console shows spike-report's lines and "Run ... has ended": press a key to close
it, and go on to the next run, or stop there.

1. run-S0A-early.cmd: S0-A, loaded early (about 30 minutes, unattended)

   The launcher starts the game twenty times, loads EldenNet as it starts, waits until EldenNet's start is over (today:
   its banner at the title screen), and closes the game again by itself. Do not press anything; you can leave the PC.
   The panel shows which run is under way. If a run hangs, the launcher closes it after three minutes.

2. run-S0A-late.cmd: S0-A, loaded late (about 30 minutes, unattended)

   The same, with EldenNet loaded at the title screen, about fifteen seconds after the game's window shows.

3. run-S0B.cmd: S0-B, the reader (a few minutes today; two hours once the table is complete)

     1. Wait at the title screen       until the row turns grey (about a minute)
     2. Load your save                 press Pause when you stand in the world
     3. Play for two hours             as you usually do: the open world, a dungeon, fights, a death or two, fast
                                       travel and the menus; the panel counts the time down
     4. Quit the game

   Today the first task ends with the console's line "The start's layout is not complete yet: S0-B waits for it.", and
   the panel then shows one task, Quit the game. Do that: the run is done. Once the table is complete, the run goes on
   to the save and the two hours; you may stop early by quitting the game, and the report says how long EldenNet read.

4. run-S0C-file.cmd and 5. run-S0C-env.cmd: S0-C, offline play (about 10 minutes each)

   The first starts the game with the launcher's usual way of starting it offline (the steam_appid.txt file beside the
   game while it starts), the second with the same id given to the game in its environment instead.

     1. Wait at the title screen
     2. Look at the title screen's menu  note whether it offers online play or shows a message about the network, then
                                         press Pause
     3. Load your save                   press Pause when you stand in the world
     4. Play for five minutes            open the game's menu once and look at its network settings without changing
                                         them: note whether online play is offered
     5. Quit the game

6. run-S0D.cmd and 7. run-S0D-veh.cmd: S0-D, guarded reads (about 15 minutes each)

   The second does the same as the first, with vehprobe.dll loaded beside EldenNet.

     1. Wait at the title screen       EldenNet makes 40 of its own reads fail on purpose here, and catches them
     2. Load your save                 press Pause when you stand in the world
     3. Open the Steam overlay once    press Shift+Tab, wait a moment, press Shift+Tab again, then press Pause; if the
                                       Steam overlay does not show in this game, just press Pause
     4. Play for ten minutes           as you usually do; the game should not notice anything
     5. Quit the game

8. run-S0D-crash.cmd: S0-D's crashes on purpose (about 10 minutes, unattended)

   Five starts. Once EldenNet's start is over in each, the launcher asks it to crash the game, and checks that the crash
   was recorded. The game closing with an error, or Windows saying it stopped working, is expected here: close any such
   message. Do not press anything else.

If you have them, the rows beside other software are runs of their own, with the same files: S0-A and S0-D beside
Seamless Co-op (with a partner, for S0-C too) or Reforged, and beside a crash reporter of your own. Tell us which you
ran. They are optional: a row not run is noted and run later.


What to send back
-----------------

  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\spikes\                   the whole folder: required
  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\eldennet.log         helpful
  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\launcher.log         helpful
  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\crashes\                  the newest file there, only if the game crashed outside run S0D-crash

(Type %APPDATA%\EldenNet into Explorer's address bar to open the folder.) The spikes folder is small. It holds the
launcher's log of each run, a folder for each start with EldenNet's report of it (eldennet-spike.json), and the
spikes' reports that the run files wrote (report-s0a.json, report-s0b.json, report-s0c.json, report-s0d.json). Each
of them holds only numbers and fixed words. You can check one yourself:

  eldennet-engine.exe lint --report "%APPDATA%\EldenNet\spikes\report-s0a.json"

It must say "lint: the report is clean".

Keep %APPDATA%\EldenNet\recordings\ on your PC: it holds the sessions' recordings (numbers only), which can be large.
We may ask for a few numbers from them later; spike-report has read them already.

With them, your answers to:

  - S0-C, each run: did the title screen's menu offer online play, or show a message about the network? Did the game's
    network settings offer online play?
  - S0-D: did the Steam overlay show? Did the game stutter, freeze or behave oddly at any time?
  - Anything else you noticed: a stutter, a freeze, a message, a run that did not end by itself.


What it does
------------

  - Backs up your saves (it only copies them), checks that Steam is running and that the game is not, and checks that
    your game is the build the tables are for (2.7.2.0, worldwide). If the launcher says the build is not supported,
    stop: the game has been updated since, and the tables must be found again first.
  - Starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat: by writing the game's Steam app id into steam_appid.txt beside
    the game while it starts, and removing it again (eldennet-launcher.exe --cleanup removes it if a crash left it),
    or, in run S0C-env, by giving the game that id in its environment. For each start it makes a folder of its own in
    %APPDATA%\EldenNet\spikes, named by the spike and a number, and names it to EldenNet.
  - Loads eldennet.dll into the game (and, in run S0D-veh, vehprobe.dll first). In the game, EldenNet reads the game's
    code and data as they are loaded, read-only and through guarded copies. It takes each place its table holds and
    checks it there, searching for nothing the table holds; once its start is over it checks them all again. Today
    its start then stops, since a place it needs is not in the table yet, and shows its banner.
  - Once the table is complete: it reads the game from a thread of its own, about ten times a second, and counts what
    it read, the readings it dropped because the game changed something while it read, and the reads that failed.
  - In S0-D: it tries forty copies of memory that cannot be read (twenty with each of its two ways of copying: a small
    piece of its own memory that it has made unreadable, and the address no memory ever has), and counts how many
    failures it caught; nothing of the game is read for that.
    Once the table is complete, it also makes one group of its own reads of the game fail, until that group is
    switched off. In S0D-crash, it crashes the game on purpose when the launcher asks, once its start is over.
  - In S0-B, S0-C and S0-D it records the session: numbers only, in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\recordings, whatever your
    settings say.
  - Writes its report of each start into the start's folder (eldennet-spike.json), and again every ten seconds while
    the game runs: how its start ended and why, the table's places checked and how many passed, the faults made and
    caught, and the reader's counts. Numbers and fixed words only: no names of the game's, no text, no paths, nothing
    of your character or your PC.
  - The panel: a small window of the launcher's own (not of the game), always on top, that never takes the keyboard.
    The launcher asks Windows for the Pause key while a run lasts (a system hotkey: Pause goes to the launcher and not
    to the game meanwhile) and gives it back at the end. When you confirm "Load your save" or the look at the title
    menu, the launcher writes the moment into the small block of memory it shares with EldenNet, and the recording
    keeps it.
  - Watches the game until it ends, and keeps a short record if it ends badly: the error's code, which module it was
    in and the time, never a memory dump.
  - After the game has ended, the run's file runs eldennet-engine.exe spike-report: it reads the spikes folder and the
    recordings, applies the spike's pass rules, prints each rule and the verdict (pass, fail, or incomplete when
    something could not be measured yet), and writes report-<spike>.json into the spikes folder.


What it never does
------------------

  - It never calls anything in the game, never adds anything to the game's own lists, never hooks anything, and never
    changes the game's code or data. It reads them.
  - It never goes online, and the game cannot reach FromSoftware's servers without Easy Anti-Cheat.
  - It never changes your saves or the game's files (steam_appid.txt aside, which it removes).
  - It never runs anything else, uses the network, keeps keys or sends anything anywhere. You send the files yourself.


If the game crashes or freezes
------------------------------

  - In run S0D-crash the crashes are meant. Anywhere else, note when it happened.
  - Your saves were backed up before the game started: each backup is a folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups,
    named by date and time, with a manifest of the files' sizes and hashes. The game's own save is not touched by
    EldenNet; only if the game itself reports a damaged save, copy ER0000.sl2 (and ER0000.sl2.bak) from the newest
    backup back into %APPDATA%\EldenRing\<the same number>\, with the game closed.
  - If the game freezes, close it with Alt+F4, or end eldenring.exe in Task Manager.
  - Send the files above, the newest crash record included. The next start of the launcher explains it once.
  - To play normally again, start the game from Steam as usual. Nothing of EldenNet stays loaded.


Check the files
---------------

In PowerShell, in this folder:

  Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 eldennet-launcher.exe, eldennet.dll, eldennet-engine.exe, vehprobe.dll

Each value must equal its line in SHA256SUMS (letter case aside).


